module

public import LeanCategories.Catalogue.Registry.Semantic

/-!
# Route discovery: retained comparison witnesses and the finiteness obligation

Issue #99, read with its later corrections (comments 6474191513, 6474821557, 6475500228,
6476290045), withdraws three claims this module therefore does not make: that routes are the path
category quotiented by the relation generated by the registered invertible cells (a quotient forgets
which cell was selected); that finiteness of the quiver, or a non-increasing well-founded measure
alone, yields finitely many routes (a loop repeats, and finite sublevel sets are a further
hypothesis); and that ambiguity is decidable by the registered relation (equality in a category
presented by generators and relations is the word problem). What the issue does owe is here:

* `ComparisonWitness`, `SemanticState.comparisonCandidates`, `SemanticState.selectComparison`:
  the registered comparison cells relating two routes, selected as *data*. Each witness retains the
  whole registered row (its stable id, its declaration, its own two routes and its endpoints),
  the direction it is read in, and the context it is read in, so that two parallel cells are two
  candidates (`comparisonCandidates_pair_retained`) and a pair related by two of them is reported
  as `competing`, never resolved by precedence (`selectComparison_eq_competing_of_two`). Selection
  reads the finite array of registered rows: it decides nothing about routes that no registered
  cell relates, which is why `ComparisonSelection.unrelated` is not a claim of inequality.
* `Reaches`, `finite_reach_of_finite_sublevels`, `finite_reach_of_enclosure`,
  `finite_reach_of_finite_edges`: reachability of categories along the search's own steps, proved
  from a *named* finiteness hypothesis -- finite sublevel sets of a strictly decreasing measure, an
  independently checked finite enclosure, or an edge presentation by finitely many edges, in which
  case the enclosure is checked by the presentation itself. `counterStep_reach_infinite` and
  `counterStep_not_finite_sublevels` show the measure hypothesis is not redundant: a strictly
  decreasing measure whose sublevel sets are infinite reaches an infinite set.
* `walk_length_le_card`: under a checked enclosure every simple walk is no longer than the
  enclosure, which is what turns a fuel bound from a resource safeguard into a consequence of a
  stated hypothesis. Without that hypothesis the safeguard stays: `SemanticState.routes` runs on
  `fuel : Nat := 32`, and the kernel enumerator's `limit := 4096` on reachable categories is a
  bound of the same kind. Neither is replaced here, and this module proves no theorem about
  reachable categories of a registry beyond the trivial one that the realizations admit.

Explicitly specified composites are a separate matter from implicit simple-route discovery:
`SemanticState.routeEdges?` checks a given list of steps against the structural graph edge by edge,
with no simplicity restriction and no fuel, so the enumeration's restrictions cannot reject one.
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace CasCatalogue

/-! ## Comparison selection: exact witnesses, direction, context -/

/-- The direction in which a registered comparison cell is read between two routes. -/
inductive ComparisonDirection
  /-- As registered: the cell's own `left` route is read as the `left` route. -/
  | forward
  /-- Reversed. Only an invertible cell (a natural isomorphism) may be read this way. -/
  | backward
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr, Inhabited

/-- The context in which a registered cell is read. A registered cell is a 2-cell of `Cat`, and it
may be used after whiskering by a route or as the image of a functorial constructor's action; the
context records which, so that one cell read in two contexts yields two distinguishable witnesses.
The legality of a context (that a whiskering route ends where the cell begins) belongs to the
composite that is being built, not to selection. -/
inductive ComparisonContext
  /-- The cell at its own endpoints. -/
  | direct
  /-- The cell whiskered on the left by a chosen route ending where the cell begins. -/
  | whiskerLeft (before : Array EdgeRef)
  /-- The cell whiskered on the right by a chosen route starting where the cell ends. -/
  | whiskerRight (after : Array EdgeRef)
  /-- The image of the cell under a functorial constructor's action (`Arr(−)`, `Core(−)`). -/
  | constructMap (constructor : ConstructorId)
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr, Inhabited

/-- One registered comparison cell selected for a pair of routes. The cell is retained whole: its
stable id, its declaration (whose parameter telescope `validateCell` checks at registration, and
which selection never instantiates), its own `left` and `right` routes and its endpoints. Nothing
here identifies two routes by equality in a presented category. -/
structure ComparisonWitness where
  /-- The registered row, exactly as registered. -/
  cell : CellEntry
  /-- The direction the witness is read in. -/
  direction : ComparisonDirection
  /-- The context the witness is read in. -/
  context : ComparisonContext := .direct
  deriving Repr

/-- What selection reports for a pair of routes. `unrelated` means no registered cell relates
them, which is not a proof that they differ: the issue's own scope statement, that ambiguity is
the word problem for a presented category, is why no such proof is offered. -/
inductive ComparisonSelection
  /-- No registered cell relates the pair. -/
  | unrelated
  /-- Exactly one registered cell relates the pair; that cell, with its direction. -/
  | unique (witness : ComparisonWitness)
  /-- Several registered cells relate the pair. All are retained, and none is preferred. -/
  | competing (witnesses : List ComparisonWitness)
  deriving Repr

namespace ComparisonSelection

def isUnrelated : ComparisonSelection → Bool
  | .unrelated => true
  | _ => false

def isUnique : ComparisonSelection → Bool
  | .unique _ => true
  | _ => false

def isCompeting : ComparisonSelection → Bool
  | .competing _ => true
  | _ => false

end ComparisonSelection

/-- The registered cell rows, as a list. -/
def SemanticState.registeredCells (state : SemanticState) : List CellEntry := state.cells.toList

/-- Whether a registered cell relates two routes in the stated orientation. -/
def CellEntry.relates (cell : CellEntry) (left right : Array EdgeRef) : Bool :=
  decide (cell.left = left ∧ cell.right = right)

theorem CellEntry.relates_eq_true {cell : CellEntry} {left right : Array EdgeRef} :
    cell.relates left right = true ↔ cell.left = left ∧ cell.right = right := by
  simp [CellEntry.relates, decide_eq_true_eq]

/-- The registered cells relating `left` and `right`, in the orientation that reads `left` first.
A cell registered `left ⟶ right` is a `.forward` witness; an invertible cell registered
`right ⟶ left` is a `.backward` witness. A non-invertible cell is never read backwards, and no two
candidates are merged, so parallel cells stay two entries. -/
def SemanticState.comparisonCandidates (state : SemanticState) (left right : Array EdgeRef) :
    List ComparisonWitness :=
  ((state.registeredCells.filter fun cell => cell.relates left right).map fun cell =>
      { cell, direction := .forward }) ++
    ((state.registeredCells.filter fun cell => cell.invertible && cell.relates right left).map
      fun cell => { cell, direction := .backward })

/-- Whether a witness is a candidate for the pair: its registered row relates the routes in the
direction it is read, or it is an invertible row read backwards. -/
def SemanticState.IsComparisonCandidate (state : SemanticState) (left right : Array EdgeRef)
    (witness : ComparisonWitness) : Prop :=
  witness.cell ∈ state.registeredCells ∧ witness.context = .direct ∧
    ((witness.direction = .forward ∧ witness.cell.left = left ∧
        witness.cell.right = right) ∨
      (witness.direction = .backward ∧ witness.cell.invertible = true ∧
        witness.cell.left = right ∧ witness.cell.right = left))

/-- A witness is the structure of its own three fields, which is how a candidate is rebuilt from its
registered row: retained, never reconstructed, in either direction. -/
theorem ComparisonWitness.mk_eq {cell : CellEntry} {witness : ComparisonWitness}
    {direction : ComparisonDirection} (hcell : witness.cell = cell)
    (hdirection : witness.direction = direction) (hcontext : witness.context = .direct) :
    ({ cell := cell, direction := direction, context := ComparisonContext.direct }
      : ComparisonWitness) = witness := by
  simp only [← hcell, ← hdirection, ← hcontext]

/-- Every candidate is a registered row, read in a direction that row supports; and every registered
row that relates the pair, in a supported direction, is a candidate. Parallel cells are therefore
kept as two, and none is dropped. -/
theorem mem_comparisonCandidates_iff {state : SemanticState} {left right : Array EdgeRef}
    {witness : ComparisonWitness} :
    witness ∈ state.comparisonCandidates left right ↔
      state.IsComparisonCandidate left right witness := by
  unfold SemanticState.IsComparisonCandidate
  constructor
  · intro hmem
    rcases List.mem_append.mp hmem with hforward | hbackward
    · rcases List.mem_map.mp hforward with ⟨cell, hcellMem, heq⟩
      rw [List.mem_filter] at hcellMem
      obtain ⟨hleft, hright⟩ := CellEntry.relates_eq_true.mp hcellMem.2
      rw [← heq]
      exact ⟨hcellMem.1, rfl, Or.inl ⟨rfl, hleft, hright⟩⟩
    · rcases List.mem_map.mp hbackward with ⟨cell, hcellMem, heq⟩
      rw [List.mem_filter] at hcellMem
      obtain ⟨hinvertible, hrelates⟩ :
          cell.invertible = true ∧ cell.relates right left = true := by
        simpa using hcellMem.2
      obtain ⟨hleft, hright⟩ := CellEntry.relates_eq_true.mp hrelates
      rw [← heq]
      exact ⟨hcellMem.1, rfl, Or.inr ⟨rfl, hinvertible, hleft, hright⟩⟩
  · rintro ⟨hmem, hcontext, hforward | hbackward⟩
    · obtain ⟨hforward, hleft, hright⟩ := hforward
      refine List.mem_append.mpr (Or.inl (List.mem_map.mpr ⟨witness.cell, ?_, ?_⟩))
      · rw [List.mem_filter]
        exact ⟨hmem, CellEntry.relates_eq_true.mpr ⟨hleft, hright⟩⟩
      · exact ComparisonWitness.mk_eq rfl hforward hcontext
    · obtain ⟨hbackward, hinvertible, hleft, hright⟩ := hbackward
      refine List.mem_append.mpr (Or.inr (List.mem_map.mpr ⟨witness.cell, ?_, ?_⟩))
      · rw [List.mem_filter]
        exact ⟨hmem, by simp [hinvertible, CellEntry.relates_eq_true.mpr ⟨hleft, hright⟩]⟩
      · exact ComparisonWitness.mk_eq rfl hbackward hcontext

/-- A candidate retains its registered row: selection reconstructs no cell. -/
theorem comparisonCandidates_cell_mem {state : SemanticState} {left right : Array EdgeRef}
    {witness : ComparisonWitness} (h : witness ∈ state.comparisonCandidates left right) :
    witness.cell ∈ state.registeredCells :=
  (mem_comparisonCandidates_iff.mp h).1

/-- A backward witness is an invertible cell: direction is retained, and a natural transformation
that is not an isomorphism is never read in the reverse direction. -/
theorem comparisonCandidates_backward_invertible {state : SemanticState}
    {left right : Array EdgeRef} {witness : ComparisonWitness}
    (h : witness ∈ state.comparisonCandidates left right)
    (hdirection : witness.direction = .backward) : witness.cell.invertible = true := by
  rcases (mem_comparisonCandidates_iff.mp h).2.2 with
    ⟨hforward, -, -⟩ | ⟨-, hinvertible, -, -⟩
  · rw [hforward] at hdirection
    exact absurd hdirection (by decide)
  · exact hinvertible

/-- Distinct parallel registered cells are retained as distinct candidates: competing witnesses are
never quotiented into one, and neither is dropped. -/
theorem comparisonCandidates_pair_retained {state : SemanticState} {left right : Array EdgeRef}
    {first second : CellEntry} (hfirst : first ∈ state.registeredCells)
    (hsecond : second ∈ state.registeredCells) (hdistinct : first.id ≠ second.id)
    (hfirstLeft : first.left = left) (hfirstRight : first.right = right)
    (hsecondLeft : second.left = left) (hsecondRight : second.right = right) :
    ∃ a ∈ state.comparisonCandidates left right,
      ∃ b ∈ state.comparisonCandidates left right, a.cell.id ≠ b.cell.id := by
  refine ⟨⟨first, .forward, .direct⟩, mem_comparisonCandidates_iff.mpr ⟨hfirst, rfl,
    Or.inl ⟨rfl, hfirstLeft, hfirstRight⟩⟩, ⟨second, .forward, .direct⟩,
    mem_comparisonCandidates_iff.mpr ⟨hsecond, rfl,
      Or.inl ⟨rfl, hsecondLeft, hsecondRight⟩⟩, ?_⟩
  simpa using hdistinct

/-- Selection: no registered comparison, a unique witness, or the competing witnesses themselves.
Nothing is ordered and nothing is chosen by precedence. -/
def SemanticState.selectComparison (state : SemanticState) (left right : Array EdgeRef) :
    ComparisonSelection :=
  match state.comparisonCandidates left right with
  | [] => .unrelated
  | [witness] => .unique witness
  | witnesses => .competing witnesses

theorem selectComparison_eq_unrelated_iff {state : SemanticState} {left right : Array EdgeRef} :
    state.selectComparison left right = .unrelated ↔
      state.comparisonCandidates left right = [] := by
  cases h : state.comparisonCandidates left right with
  | nil => simp [SemanticState.selectComparison, h]
  | cons head tail =>
      cases tail with
      | nil => simp [SemanticState.selectComparison, h]
      | cons second rest => simp [SemanticState.selectComparison, h]

theorem selectComparison_eq_unique_iff {state : SemanticState} {left right : Array EdgeRef}
    {witness : ComparisonWitness} :
    state.selectComparison left right = .unique witness ↔
      state.comparisonCandidates left right = [witness] := by
  cases h : state.comparisonCandidates left right with
  | nil => simp [SemanticState.selectComparison, h]
  | cons head tail =>
      cases tail with
      | nil => simp [SemanticState.selectComparison, h]
      | cons second rest => simp [SemanticState.selectComparison, h]

/-- Two distinct registered cells relating the same pair give the competing outcome, and both are
in the reported list: the ambiguity is reported, not decided. -/
theorem selectComparison_eq_competing_of_two {state : SemanticState} {left right : Array EdgeRef}
    {first second : ComparisonWitness}
    (hfirst : first ∈ state.comparisonCandidates left right)
    (hsecond : second ∈ state.comparisonCandidates left right)
    (hdistinct : first.cell.id ≠ second.cell.id) :
    ∃ witnesses : List ComparisonWitness,
      state.selectComparison left right = .competing witnesses ∧
        first ∈ witnesses ∧ second ∈ witnesses := by
  have hnempty : state.comparisonCandidates left right ≠ [] := by
    intro hempty
    rw [hempty] at hfirst
    simp at hfirst
  have hsingleton : ∀ witness : ComparisonWitness,
      state.comparisonCandidates left right ≠ [witness] := by
    intro witness hsingle
    have hfirstEq : first = witness := by
      rw [hsingle] at hfirst
      simpa using hfirst
    have hsecondEq : second = witness := by
      rw [hsingle] at hsecond
      simpa using hsecond
    exact hdistinct (by rw [hfirstEq, hsecondEq])
  cases hcandidates : state.comparisonCandidates left right with
  | nil => exact absurd hcandidates hnempty
  | cons head tail =>
      cases tail with
      | nil => exact absurd hcandidates (hsingleton head)
      | cons next rest =>
          exact ⟨head :: next :: rest,
            by simp [SemanticState.selectComparison, hcandidates],
            by rw [hcandidates] at hfirst; simpa using hfirst,
            by rw [hcandidates] at hsecond; simpa using hsecond⟩

/-- The routes a witness relates are the registered row's, in the direction it is read. -/
theorem comparisonCandidates_routes_retained {state : SemanticState} {left right : Array EdgeRef}
    {witness : ComparisonWitness} (h : witness ∈ state.comparisonCandidates left right) :
    (witness.direction = .forward ∧ witness.cell.left = left ∧ witness.cell.right = right) ∨
      (witness.direction = .backward ∧ witness.cell.left = right ∧
        witness.cell.right = left) := by
  rcases (mem_comparisonCandidates_iff.mp h).2.2 with ⟨hforward, hleft, hright⟩ |
    ⟨hbackward, -, hleft, hright⟩
  · exact Or.inl ⟨hforward, hleft, hright⟩
  · exact Or.inr ⟨hbackward, hleft, hright⟩

/-- A route retains the selected edge sequence and each step's exact label. -/
theorem Route.refs_retains_steps (route : Route) : route.steps.map (·.ref) = route.refs := rfl

/-! ## The finiteness obligation -/

namespace RouteDiscovery

variable {V : Type*}

/-- `Reaches Step s v`: `v` is reached from `s` by a finite chain of steps. -/
def Reaches (Step : V → V → Prop) (s v : V) : Prop := Relation.ReflTransGen Step s v

/-- A measure strictly decreasing along steps bounds every reached point by its value at the
source. This is the whole content of the measure: it is a bound, not a finiteness statement. -/
theorem reaches_measure_le {Step : V → V → Prop} {measure : V → ℕ}
    (hstep : ∀ ⦃u v⦄, Step u v → measure v < measure u) {s v : V}
    (h : Reaches Step s v) : measure v ≤ measure s := by
  unfold Reaches at h
  induction h with
  | refl => exact le_rfl
  | tail _ hlast ih => exact le_trans (le_of_lt (hstep hlast)) ih

/-- Finite sublevel sets of the measure: the finiteness hypothesis the measure needs. -/
def FiniteSublevels {V : Type*} (measure : V → ℕ) : Prop := ∀ n, {v | measure v ≤ n}.Finite

/-- Reachability is finite under a strictly decreasing measure whose sublevel sets are finite. -/
theorem finite_reach_of_finite_sublevels {Step : V → V → Prop} {measure : V → ℕ}
    (hstep : ∀ ⦃u v⦄, Step u v → measure v < measure u) (hfinite : FiniteSublevels measure)
    (s : V) : {v | Reaches Step s v}.Finite :=
  (hfinite (measure s)).subset fun _ hv => reaches_measure_le hstep hv

/-- Reachability is finite under an independently checked finite enclosure: the hypothesis names
the finiteness argument instead of hiding it in a measure. -/
theorem finite_reach_of_enclosure {Step : V → V → Prop} {s : V} {enclosure : Set V}
    (hfinite : enclosure.Finite) (hencloses : ∀ v, Reaches Step s v → v ∈ enclosure) :
    {v | Reaches Step s v}.Finite :=
  hfinite.subset hencloses

/-- Every point reached along finitely many edges lies in the source together with the targets of
those edges. This is the checked enclosure of a finitely presented quiver. -/
theorem mem_insert_targets_of_reaches [DecidableEq V] {Step : V → V → Prop}
    {edges : Finset (V × V)}
    (hcontained : ∀ ⦃u v⦄, Step u v → (u, v) ∈ edges) {s v : V} (hv : Reaches Step s v) :
    v ∈ insert s (edges.image Prod.snd) := by
  unfold Reaches at hv
  induction hv with
  | refl => exact Finset.mem_insert_self _ _
  | tail _ hlast ih =>
      exact Finset.mem_insert_of_mem (Finset.mem_image_of_mem Prod.snd (hcontained hlast))

/-- Reachability is finite when the step relation is presented by finitely many edges, and the
enclosure is then checked by the presentation itself. This is the form the registry's own graph
would have if its edge set were fixed; it is not, see `SemanticState.RouteStep`. -/
theorem finite_reach_of_finite_edges {Step : V → V → Prop} (edges : Finset (V × V))
    (hcontained : ∀ ⦃u v⦄, Step u v → (u, v) ∈ edges) (s : V) :
    {v | Reaches Step s v}.Finite := by
  classical
  exact (Finset.finite_toSet _).subset fun _ hv =>
    mem_insert_targets_of_reaches hcontained hv


/-- A step relation whose measure `Prod.fst` strictly decreases: each point reaches every point of
every lower level. -/
def counterStep : ℕ × ℕ → ℕ × ℕ → Prop := fun u v => v.1 < u.1

theorem counterStep_strict {u v : ℕ × ℕ} (h : counterStep u v) : v.1 < u.1 := h

/-- The measure alone is not an adequate finiteness argument: `counterStep` strictly decreases
`Prod.fst` and what `(1, 0)` reaches is infinite, because one point reaches every point of the
level below it. -/
theorem counterStep_reach_infinite :
    ¬ {v : ℕ × ℕ | Reaches counterStep (1, 0) v}.Finite := by
  intro hfinite
  have hinjective : Function.Injective fun level : ℕ => (0, level) := by
    intro left right heq
    exact congrArg Prod.snd heq
  have hmem : ∀ level : ℕ, (0, level) ∈ {v : ℕ × ℕ | Reaches counterStep (1, 0) v} := by
    intro level
    exact Relation.ReflTransGen.single (by simp [counterStep])
  exact (Set.infinite_of_injective_forall_mem hinjective hmem) hfinite

/-- The same specimen fails the sublevel hypothesis: its sublevel sets are infinite, which is why
the previous theorem is not a defect of the formulation. -/
theorem counterStep_not_finite_sublevels : ¬ FiniteSublevels (fun v : ℕ × ℕ => v.1) := by
  intro hfinite
  refine counterStep_reach_infinite ((hfinite 1).subset ?_)
  intro v hv
  exact reaches_measure_le (Step := counterStep) (measure := fun v : ℕ × ℕ => v.1)
    (fun _ _ hlast => counterStep_strict hlast) hv

/-- `IsWalk Step l`: consecutive entries of `l` are joined by `Step`. -/
def IsWalk (Step : V → V → Prop) (l : List V) : Prop :=
  ∀ (i : ℕ) (h : i + 1 < l.length), Step l[i] l[i + 1]

/-- Every entry of a walk from `s` is reachable from `s`. -/
theorem reaches_getElem_of_isWalk {Step : V → V → Prop} {s : V} {l : List V}
    (hstart : l.head? = some s) (hwalk : IsWalk Step l) :
    ∀ (i : ℕ) (hi : i < l.length), Reaches Step s l[i] := by
  intro i
  induction i with
  | zero =>
      intro _
      obtain ⟨rest, hcons⟩ := List.head?_eq_some_iff.mp hstart
      subst hcons
      exact Relation.ReflTransGen.refl
  | succ i ih =>
      intro hi
      exact (ih (by omega)).tail (hwalk i hi)

/-- Under a checked enclosure, every simple walk from `s` is no longer than the enclosure. This is
the finiteness hypothesis' payoff: it makes a fuel bound a consequence rather than a safeguard. -/
theorem walk_length_le_card {Step : V → V → Prop} {s : V} {l : List V}
    {enclosure : Finset V}
    (hstart : l.head? = some s) (hwalk : IsWalk Step l) (hnodup : l.Nodup)
    (hencloses : ∀ v, Reaches Step s v → v ∈ enclosure) : l.length ≤ enclosure.card := by
  classical
  have hsubset : l.toFinset ⊆ enclosure := by
    intro v hv
    obtain ⟨i, hi, rfl⟩ := List.getElem_of_mem (List.mem_toFinset.mp hv)
    exact hencloses _ (reaches_getElem_of_isWalk hstart hwalk i hi)
  calc l.length = l.toFinset.card := (List.toFinset_card_of_nodup hnodup).symm
    _ ≤ enclosure.card := Finset.card_le_card hsubset

/-- Under a finitely presented edge set, every simple walk from `s` is no longer than the enclosure
of that presentation: this is the fuel bound the enumeration would inherit from a finiteness
argument, rather than from a constant. -/
theorem walk_length_le_card_of_finite_edges [DecidableEq V] {Step : V → V → Prop}
    (edges : Finset (V × V)) (hcontained : ∀ ⦃u v⦄, Step u v → (u, v) ∈ edges) {s : V}
    {l : List V} (hstart : l.head? = some s) (hwalk : IsWalk Step l) (hnodup : l.Nodup) :
    l.length ≤ (insert s (edges.image Prod.snd)).card := by
  exact walk_length_le_card hstart hwalk hnodup fun _ hv =>
    mem_insert_targets_of_reaches hcontained hv

end RouteDiscovery

/-! ## The route search's own step relation -/

/-- The one-step relation of the route search on category expressions: a structural edge out of `u`
-- registered, classifier-forgetful or constructor-derived -- whose target is `v`. -/
def SemanticState.RouteStep (state : SemanticState) (u v : CategoryExpr) : Prop :=
  ∃ edge : StructuralEdge, edge ∈ state.edgesFrom u ∧ edge.target.syntacticEq v = true

/-- Reachability in the search's graph is finite under a checked finite enclosure of the reached
category expressions. The enclosure is a hypothesis and is discharged nowhere here: the search
itself walks simple paths (`SemanticState.routes`, `fuel : Nat := 32`) and the kernel enumerator
bounds reachable categories by `limit := 4096`; both are resource bounds, and neither is a proof of
this hypothesis. -/
theorem SemanticState.finite_reach_of_enclosure (state : SemanticState) (source : CategoryExpr)
    {enclosure : Set CategoryExpr} (hfinite : enclosure.Finite)
    (hencloses : ∀ v, RouteDiscovery.Reaches state.RouteStep source v → v ∈ enclosure) :
    {v | RouteDiscovery.Reaches state.RouteStep source v}.Finite :=
  RouteDiscovery.finite_reach_of_enclosure hfinite hencloses

end CasCatalogue
